00:06:35.703 --> 00:06:37.022
So I'm just happy to be here.

00:06:37.022 --> 00:06:39.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's been
a long time, so thank you.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:40.330
Thank you so much for joining.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:41.580
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.210
So we got you in today.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.870
We want to talk
about OCTO, what it

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.800
is, what that relationship's
like with the customer.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.390
Before we do that,
can you tell us

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:52.240
a little bit about what you do
with Google, like your history?

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:53.680
Like, what do you do here?

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:54.638
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Sure.

00:06:54.638 --> 00:06:59.300
So I've been at Google now
just around five years.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.780
I used to say I joined Google
Cloud Platform before there

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.000
was a Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.190
When I joined, we had App Engine
and BigQuery and App Engine

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.100
and BigQuery.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:10.727
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:10.727 --> 00:07:11.810
MARK MANDEL: No Datastore?

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.690
Was there no Datastore then?

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:14.450
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Well, we had Datastore.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.741
I always think of that as
really being-- at that point,

00:07:16.741 --> 00:07:20.067
Datastore was only
usable from App Engine.

00:07:20.067 --> 00:07:21.400
So I sort of think of it as one.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.820
I'm still the hold over that
forgets that Datastore now

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.500
actually can be used
from any product.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:31.600
So yeah, I started back
at the beginning of GCP.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.660
For the first few years,
I led product management

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:38.860
for the compute side, so
App Engine, Compute Engine,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.920
Kubernetes, the billing
system, the tools, the SDKs,

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.400
that sort of stuff.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.610
And then as we've grown,
it's just been amazing.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:50.150
And about, well, I guess,
a year ago, we saw,

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:53.410
with Diane Green joining
to run the organization,

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:58.600
we saw this huge increase in the
number of really big customers,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:02.110
brands that anybody out there
would recognize that wanted

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:03.610
to talk about Google Cloud.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.410
And they needed
somebody, or a group

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.030
of people, who were technical.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:08.950
I mean, I'm an engineer
by training, right?

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.290
In between this meeting,
I was compiling TensorFlow

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.720
from sources on my
laptop a couple of hours

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.330
ago to get one
that was optimized

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.740
for my particular processor.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.720
So I'm an engineer at heart.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.810
But I've done this
long enough that I also

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:26.980
understand what businesses and
enterprises and C-level execs

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:27.740
think and want.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.500
And so I view my role and I
view the CTO's office role,

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:33.580
it's really two ways.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.580
I mean, you know like you guys,
you're developer advocates.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:41.559
Your job is to advocate for
Google to developers but then

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:43.840
also to do the reverse,
right-- to advocate

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.719
for those developers
back to Google?

00:08:45.719 --> 00:08:46.510
MARK MANDEL: Mm-hm.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:48.926
GREG DEMICHILLIE: I kind of
view my job is the same thing.

00:08:48.926 --> 00:08:51.430
Except instead of
developers, my job

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:56.110
is to advocate for these
senior executives at some

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.360
of these big companies, right?

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.490
And just like you
don't think about one

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.420
product-- you think about
the whole platform--

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.310
it's kind of the same.

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:07.870
I don't think about just data
storage, just machine learning,

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:09.334
or just Compute Engine.

00:09:09.334 --> 00:09:10.750
I spend time with
those customers.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.640
And then I try to
help the product

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.180
teams across the
whole suite understand

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.790
that set of customers and
understand what they care about

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:20.470
and their pain points.

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:23.620
So I really think it's
very similar to Dev Rel,

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:26.860
except it's not aimed at
individual developers.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:29.860
It's aimed at the
C-level executive,

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:33.100
the senior technical people
in their organizations.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.460
And it's fun because it means
in the morning my day might

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:40.420
be one part meeting with
some SVPs of a customer

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:41.980
or a potential customer.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.760
It might be sitting in on a
tech review for a new upcoming

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.140
release of a product.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.550
And then in between,
like I said,

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.860
I was compiling TensorFlow
from sources a little bit

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:51.610
earlier today.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.567
So it's kind of fun to
be able to do all that.

00:09:54.567 --> 00:09:56.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So all
of this kind of reminds me

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:01.990
of previous episodes we've done
lately on customer engineers

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:03.425
and customer
reliability engineers

00:10:03.425 --> 00:10:06.940
and all of these people that
are kind of different kinds

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:08.430
of contacts to Google Cloud.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:08.800
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Mm-hmm.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How
are you different

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:12.110
or how are you similar
to like, for instance,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:16.640
let's say, like strategic
customer engineers?

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.450
GREG DEMICHILLIE: So I
mean the thing to remember

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:24.130
is that when you're dealing with
a company that's as big as, you

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:28.270
know, HSBC or a company
as big as, you know,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:31.300
Snapchat or Spotify,
they're filled

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:32.650
with people at every level.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:35.080
They have people who
want to talk to somebody

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:36.887
who can write code for them.

00:10:36.887 --> 00:10:38.470
They also want to
talk with people who

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.520
can think about the business.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.120
So one difference is,
the main difference

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.450
is sort of who we interact
with in the organization.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:52.600
So we tend to spend most
of our times with SVPs,

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:58.207
the CIO, the CTO, the
directors of engineering,

00:10:58.207 --> 00:10:59.290
and those sorts of things.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:04.040
We view our job is to sort
of be an advisor to them.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:05.410
We're not in sales.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:08.170
And I think that's kind of
an important distinction.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.140
Like you, we're part of
the engineering team.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.330
And so those sorts of
customers like having

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:15.070
someone they can talk
to that they know

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.250
is focused on the long term.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:21.430
Like, my job isn't to retire
quota for the sales team

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:22.510
this quarter, right?

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:25.180
My job is to think about
the two-year journey

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:31.040
this customer is on and to try
to optimize for the long term.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.000
And so that's-- and it's
helpful that we just are

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.980
in engineering, right?

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:40.390
We're that much closer to
the product development team.

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:43.270
But it's a horrible cliche
of it takes a village.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:47.800
But there's truth to it, right,
in that a customer like this

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.470
needs technical
account managers.

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.600
They need customer engineers.

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:56.560
They need-- any
big company needs

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.940
a lot of help and assistance.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.398
When you're talking
about something

00:11:59.398 --> 00:12:01.630
as big as you know
abandoning your data center

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.120
and moving to the
cloud, right, that's

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.375
a huge change for
a company to make.

00:12:06.375 --> 00:12:07.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So you mentioned

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:12.880
some really big names,
like HSBC, Snapchat, which

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.496
is kind of funny that they're
both in the same sentence

00:12:15.496 --> 00:12:16.870
because they're
so, so different.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:17.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:18.826
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Isn't that true?

00:12:18.826 --> 00:12:19.527
Isn't that true?

00:12:19.527 --> 00:12:21.110
That's what makes
the job fun, though.

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it is very amazing.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.610
Of those customers, can
you talk a little bit

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.981
about what kind of customers
we're working with.

00:12:26.981 --> 00:12:28.480
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Yeah, so obviously

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:31.540
I can talk about the
ones that are public.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:33.520
One of the fun
things about the job

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.430
is, like you, we get involved
with customers sometimes

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:37.900
really, really early.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:40.870
And for me, the fun
is the breadth of it.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:45.070
I like one minute we're working
with the Walt Disney Company,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.200
and you're talking
about, I mean, the people

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.700
who build the theme park I grew
up going to as a kid, right?

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.190
Then it might be Spotify.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.700
And what a really cool company,
with thinking about how

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:59.329
to revolutionize music.

00:12:59.329 --> 00:13:00.745
And then it'll be
a bank suddenly.

00:13:00.745 --> 00:13:03.430
And, OK, now you've got to
switch your head to thinking

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:05.364
about the way a bank works.

00:13:05.364 --> 00:13:07.780
Actually, it gives me-- it's
a good point to talk about is

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.430
the CTO office is--

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.260
it's a fairly large team.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:14.770
And I would say one
part of the team

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:17.950
are people like me,
fundamentally technologists

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.770
who've been at Google
a reasonably long time

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.380
and who understand
the whole product mix.

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:27.212
But the other part are
Brian Stephens, our CTO,

00:13:27.212 --> 00:13:28.170
uses this great phrase.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:31.120
He calls them "recovering CTOs."

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:37.300
That is they're former CTOs of
companies like GE Manufacturing

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.810
or an oil and gas company.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.870
So they come in
with this really "I

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:45.701
understand this
vertical market."

00:13:45.701 --> 00:13:47.200
I mean, in some
ways I think of them

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:48.511
as like a universal translator.

00:13:48.511 --> 00:13:49.510
Do you know what I mean?

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:53.080
Like, we speak
containers, and they

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.730
speak hydrogeological
analysis, right?

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:02.530
They can translate technology
speak into the language

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:04.420
of an industry.

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.250
So part of the fun
things about the job

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.400
is I get to work
with people who have

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.760
all sorts of really interesting
kind of crazy backgrounds.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.420
And they aren't just kind of
engineering people like me,

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:20.740
but they're also people who
come from an oil-and-gas

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:24.640
or a finance or a
manufacturing background.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:27.100
And so that means that
we work with companies

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.420
all over the board
and worldwide.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.500
So for me, that's the
fun part of the job.

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:37.570
Even as an individual
engineer, I liked the demo.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:40.525
I liked showing off what
you'd built to somebody.

00:14:40.525 --> 00:14:41.650
Do you know what I mean?

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:43.434
Like when you build
something cool

00:14:43.434 --> 00:14:45.100
and you show it to a
customer or a user,

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:47.830
and you see them go,
oh, wow, that's neat.

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.920
That, to me, was always the
really fun part of the job.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.405
So in some ways now, I get
nothing but the fun part.

00:14:53.405 --> 00:14:54.280
You know what I mean?

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.234
So it's perfect for me.

00:14:56.234 --> 00:14:58.150
MARK MANDEL: No, that
sounds very interesting.

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:00.295
So I guess that
means then-- like you

00:15:00.295 --> 00:15:01.535
were talking about people
who have verticalized.

00:15:01.535 --> 00:15:04.350
So maybe if you're working with,
like, a large banking customer,

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.500
you might go in to talk
about the broader picture,

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:08.995
but you might also
bring in, say,

00:15:08.995 --> 00:15:11.460
if there's somebody has a
verticalized title for finance

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:12.450
within OCTO as well.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.215
So you have that sort
of team coming in.

00:15:14.715 --> 00:15:15.630
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Well, and that's also--

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:16.080
you made a good point.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.080
You talk about bringing
other people in.

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.240
OCTO does not have a
monopoly on smart people.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:25.350
And so part of our job is to
know folks like you and folks

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.160
like the PM team and
the engineering team.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.610
So if we're having a
conversation with somebody

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.190
and it turns out somebody
in their organization really

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.230
needs to understand,
I don't know,

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.470
how the Kubernetes scheduler
works or something like that,

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:40.631
we draw on the rest of the
product and the engineering

00:15:40.631 --> 00:15:41.130
team.

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:44.520
And the good news is most of the
product and engineering people

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.940
I talk to, a chance to go sit
down with a real customer?

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.674
I mean, that's gold.

00:15:49.674 --> 00:15:51.090
When you're building
products that

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.910
are used by thousands
or millions of people,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:56.610
sometimes it can be
hard because it's

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:59.970
easy to get divorced and
separated from the actual user

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:01.320
because you don't know them.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:04.770
But if you take an engineer
and you have them sit there

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.680
in a white board with-- like,
one of our network engineers

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:12.120
sits on a whiteboard with a
network operator at Spotify.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.060
And they sketch
problems out together.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:15.710
That sticks.

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:16.710
Do you know what I mean?

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.082
Like, that encounter
sticks with that person.

00:16:19.082 --> 00:16:21.540
And when they go back and do
the rest of their development,

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.520
they will think about
that particular customer

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:24.150
they talked to.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:28.290
So we also try to
really connect the dots

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:31.680
where we can so that
the engineers get

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:35.100
that sort of direct interaction
when it makes sense.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm curious.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.270
As a customer of
Google Cloud platform,

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:44.100
what should I expect from
the office of the CTO?

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.920
Would you say it's just
a first point of contact

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.840
for them to figure
everything out,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:50.100
or what does it [INAUDIBLE]?

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:51.060
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Yeah, that's important.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.440
That's a good point.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:56.850
We're-- Google Cloud Platform
is obviously large and growing.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:58.920
We have a lot of
different customers.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.850
The CTO office doesn't
scale in that sense, right?

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:07.260
Even with a team
of 40, do the math.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:11.349
That's not big enough to
support 1,000 customers.

00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:16.980
So we have a bit of a process
to determine where we engage.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:21.359
It's largely driven by the
needs of the product teams

00:17:21.359 --> 00:17:24.359
and the needs of
the sales teams.

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:29.070
Our primary focus is on those,
call it, the 500, 1,000,

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.160
the biggest companies, the
biggest brands and not just

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:35.801
by revenue, but in terms of
their brand and their brand

00:17:35.801 --> 00:17:36.300
value.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.883
Obviously, there are
certain customers

00:17:37.883 --> 00:17:39.870
that if we bring them
onto the platform,

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:43.120
they tell a great story
that helps other customers.

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.350
So we have a bunch of different
criteria for what we look at.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:49.034
The sad part is we can't
engage with every customer

00:17:49.034 --> 00:17:49.950
that we would want to.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:51.600
There's just not enough of us.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:55.140
So unfortunately, we have to
do a little a bit of a triage.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.540
The good news is,
though, the other part

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:59.770
of the job that I
really enjoy is many

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:01.950
of us are very active at
things like Google Cloud

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.339
Next and the summits.

00:18:04.339 --> 00:18:05.880
We were very big in
the New York one.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.354
I obviously went
to San Francisco.

00:18:08.354 --> 00:18:10.770
I'm getting ready to go to
Google Cloud Summit in Sydney--

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:11.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Me too.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:16.500
GREG DEMICHILLIE: And I'll be
in Paris and, I think, Munich.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.290
So if you come to
those events, that's

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.570
also a chance to
interact with us

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:24.060
and to get some
advice and guidance

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:27.370
and just talk to us about
Google Cloud and how that works.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: This makes
me think about during Cloud

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:33.150
Next in San Francisco,
there was actually

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:37.830
a special track for these kind
of most important or strategic

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:38.340
customers.

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:40.890
Is that something
that you also do, or--

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:43.590
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Yeah, we
work very closely with that.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.070
There's these things called
leaders circle that we run,

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.137
sort of--

00:18:48.137 --> 00:18:49.470
I don't want to say in parallel.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:54.480
But the attendees are invited
by Google to come to this.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.520
They're usually the
more senior executives.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:58.440
They will go to the keynotes.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:00.060
And then they'll
come to-- instead

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.450
of going to the
engineering code breakouts,

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:05.580
they go to their own sort
of set of breakouts that

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.712
talk about some business
issues and business strategy

00:19:08.712 --> 00:19:09.670
and that sort of stuff.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:13.320
So, yeah, we're very
involved in those.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:16.260
And again that's another
fun opportunity for us

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.600
to sort of understand
what's going on

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.550
or what these customers
actually need from us.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:22.200
So I love that part of the job.

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.562
Again, Next and Summit
and those sorts of things,

00:19:24.562 --> 00:19:26.020
I think that's the
fun part, right,

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.603
to actually get out
and talk to people

00:19:27.603 --> 00:19:28.740
and show what you've done.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.948
MARK MANDEL: Actually, now
I'm quite curious as well.

00:19:30.948 --> 00:19:33.750
As you say, you've got a
long history of engineering.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:36.690
What do you find are the
big differences between sort

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.202
of working with-- like, I know
we're developer advocates.

00:19:39.202 --> 00:19:40.410
We work at a developer level.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.951
You work at probably like
more of a CXO kind of level.

00:19:42.951 --> 00:19:44.700
What do you find that
big difference there

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.380
in terms of like
the expectations

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.610
or what people are looking for
out of either GCP or from you

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:51.439
as the office of CTO?

00:19:51.439 --> 00:19:52.980
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Well, I mean, first

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.260
what's the same is you
have to be grounded

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.390
in technical reality, right?

00:19:57.390 --> 00:20:00.120
I can't do my job
if I don't have

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.490
some understanding of
how TensorFlow works

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.060
and how Kubernetes works.

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:07.570
And I don't have any code
contributions that have ever

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:09.760
been pushed into those repos.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.521
I don't think I ever will.

00:20:11.521 --> 00:20:13.270
But it starts with you
have to be grounded

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.440
in a technical reality.

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:17.200
Because at the end of
the day, the customer's

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.900
buying technology from
you, and they want to know,

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.700
does it work?

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:23.320
Will it work to
solve their problems?

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.300
And so it starts
with, just like you,

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:28.090
that same grounded
in reality part.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.820
What's different
is my slide decks

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:33.630
don't really have
code samples anymore.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:35.170
But they have
technical material.

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:38.450
I do talk about things
like spanner scaling.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:40.610
Or I do talk about things
like machine learning,

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.360
training and how much
compute do you need.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:47.110
How much data is enough to
build a decent ML model.

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:51.590
It is technical, but I don't
have code samples and APIs

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:53.560
in my slides anymore.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:57.775
My demos aren't opening up
an IDE and editing code.

00:20:57.775 --> 00:20:58.900
They're slightly different.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:01.450
But what stays the
same is you have

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.877
to be grounded in the
technical truth in reality,

00:21:03.877 --> 00:21:05.710
because otherwise you're
not helping, right?

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:07.690
You're just selling.

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:09.569
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:21:09.569 --> 00:21:11.360
The other thing I was
curious about as well

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:14.680
is what does an engagement with
the office of CTO look like?

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:17.020
I know like Cs
obviously-- obviously

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:19.810
Cs have, like, regular
meetings and stuff like that.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:21.811
Is it the same for
Office CTO as well?

00:21:21.811 --> 00:21:23.560
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Yeah,
it's a good point.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.018
First, it's important
to understand

00:21:25.018 --> 00:21:29.290
we usually are involved early
on in the early process.

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:33.640
And then we usually phase
out a bit once the customer

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:35.180
is fully onboard.

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:36.791
Where we really try
to help is there's

00:21:36.791 --> 00:21:39.040
that point when the customer's
trying to get their sea

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:40.090
legs under them.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:40.600
Do you know what I mean?

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:42.224
They know they want
to be on the cloud.

00:21:42.224 --> 00:21:44.450
They know it's different.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.437
They don't quite
necessarily know

00:21:46.437 --> 00:21:48.520
which project should be
their first project, which

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:49.960
should be their second.

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:54.940
So we're very intensely involved
in that sort of early phase.

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:57.640
Once the customer's really
ramped up and in production

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:00.220
and they know Google Cloud
Platform really well,

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:05.230
then our interaction
phases out, because now

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:07.600
the strategic customer
engineers, all the rest

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:10.810
of Google's technical
team can help them work

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:12.740
with that customer over time.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:16.840
So we tend to be very involved
in sort of early-on phases

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:19.150
and then phasing out a bit.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.250
We stay in touch with
them because we also

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:23.990
run our customer advisory boards
and those sorts of things.

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:25.300
So we stay in touch with them.

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:29.740
But the depth of the engagement,
instead of being on the phone

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:33.760
or on GVC once a week or
flying out to London, which

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.950
in the early
phases, we literally

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:39.580
will be talking with someone
at one of these companies once

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:42.550
a week, multiple times a week,
it eventually phases out,

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:45.400
and it becomes more of a
checking in periodically.

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:46.360
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.150
And it sounds like you've helped
a lot with some fairly large

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:51.160
migrations into the cloud.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:52.744
Have there been any
particular lessons

00:22:52.744 --> 00:22:55.201
you've learned along the way
that maybe our listeners might

00:22:55.201 --> 00:22:56.260
want to be interested in?

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:57.880
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
I think right now

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:00.340
there's a lot of
block-and-tackle work

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:04.940
of migrating data and security
and all those sorts of things.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.270
But I think the biggest
one, I would say,

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.240
is when I look at companies
that migrate really quickly

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:12.710
and versus companies
that take a long time,

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:16.060
often the difference isn't
so much in the technology

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:17.110
of the company.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.840
It's in the approach
the company takes.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:22.120
The companies that
move fast, move fast

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:25.090
because there is
one or more leader

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:29.650
in the company who's committed
to making it happen fast.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:32.740
Because, look, there'll
be hiccups along the way.

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:35.140
And companies that
move fast, they

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:37.360
don't allow everything
to grind to a halt

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:38.857
when they hit that first hiccup.

00:23:38.857 --> 00:23:40.940
They sit there and say,
OK, we've got to solve it.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:42.160
Let's move that to the side.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:44.440
Let's get some smart
people on it solving it.

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:47.260
But let's keep everything
else moving forward.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:49.060
The companies that
take a long time,

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:51.370
sometimes that
stuff's like Velcro,

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.215
and you get stuck on it.

00:23:53.215 --> 00:23:54.090
You know what I mean?

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.010
And so the companies
that take longer,

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:01.660
often it's because everything
starts to sort of slow down

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:04.750
when you hit the inevitable
issues or hiccups.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:07.540
And everybody-- I mean,
companies are big.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:09.580
This stuff's complicated.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:11.800
But I think that's probably
the biggest difference

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:14.119
is you can move quickly.

00:24:14.119 --> 00:24:15.910
But as an organization,
you have to sort of

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:18.580
be committed to that, that
we're going to move quickly.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.170
When we hit
problems, we're going

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:22.570
to figure out how to
not side track them,

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.560
but work on them without
everything else grinding

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:29.080
to a halt. And that,
to me, is the pattern

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.300
I see when you see
the companies that

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.640
have really been fast moving.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:37.180
The other thing is pick a
pilot project and just start.

00:24:37.180 --> 00:24:39.640
Like, some companies
won't start anything

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:42.050
until they know everything.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:44.650
And you'll never know
everything, right?

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:45.940
Pick a pilot.

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:49.630
That pilot will teach you so
much about how the cloud works

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:52.240
and the APIs and the consoles.

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:56.020
You'll learn 10 times as much
out of that first pilot project

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.470
as your second one, because it's
when you're learning everything

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:02.380
about how all these
pieces fit together.

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:04.000
So it's credibly valuable.

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.270
So I really encourage people to
do that as early as they can,

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:10.030
because you'll come out
of that with so much more

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:13.940
knowledge about how all
these things work together.

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:16.540
So, yeah, be committed
to it and know

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:18.400
that you're going
to have issues,

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:23.230
and be committed to not letting
those make everything stop.

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:24.190
Find a pilot.

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:25.300
Start a pilot.

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:28.180
Get something so you get your
sea legs under you, right?

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:31.960
And it's not quite so
much of a strange world.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.480
And that approach,
when we see that,

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:39.410
that's when we see customers
that end up moving really fast.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:42.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you were
saying customers that move fast

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:44.860
versus customers that slow.

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:46.344
How fast is fast?

00:25:46.344 --> 00:25:48.760
GREG DEMICHILLIE: Well, I don't
remember the exact number,

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:53.500
but the Evernote, I think, is
a pretty remarkable example

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:57.670
of how quickly they moved
from there on-perm environment

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.750
to Google Cloud, including
petabytes of data.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:03.040
And as you might
imagine with Evernote,

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:06.400
they wanted to make sure
they were really satisfied

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:09.680
with security of their
customers' information

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:10.930
and all those sorts of things.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:12.580
I think they were an example.

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:14.590
Lush Cosmetics in
the UK, I think

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:18.980
the number was 28 days or
something like totally insane.

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:20.527
I'd have to go back and look.

00:26:20.527 --> 00:26:22.360
They were on stage at
Next in San Francisco,

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:25.300
and they talked about the
speed with which they moved.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.760
So, yeah, these
things can be done

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:33.310
in order of a month or
two, if you're committed,

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:37.140
and if you're all hands
on deck in that way.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:38.890
And so Evernote and
Lush were two examples

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:40.820
of companies who just did
it really, really quickly.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:43.340
Now, that doesn't mean everybody
can do it that quickly.

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:45.997
But again, in those
companies, they just--

00:26:45.997 --> 00:26:47.080
they were committed to it.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:48.780
They had an attitude
of we're going

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:51.050
to work through the
problems as they come up.

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:52.171
And we like that.

00:26:52.171 --> 00:26:53.170
Do you know what I mean?

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:55.900
Like, I think one of the
things that customers

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:59.476
that work with us find
is that at Google, I

00:26:59.476 --> 00:27:00.850
think for all of
us, we really do

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:03.470
think of this as a
partnership with the customer.

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:05.890
We don't want to build
software, throw it

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:10.630
over the wall to the customer,
good luck figuring it out.

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:13.600
We really want to have
an engineer-to-engineer

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.210
relationship with
these customers.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:19.540
And I find they appreciate
that, and that's another reason

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:20.440
they can move fast.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I think
that that is a common theme

00:27:22.900 --> 00:27:26.890
over all the episodes we've
been doing with CRE and CE

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:29.284
and [INAUDIBLE] and
all of those E's.

00:27:29.284 --> 00:27:30.700
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Yeah, it's just,

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.408
even if you're in a
customer-facing role,

00:27:32.408 --> 00:27:36.297
we hire for people
who just are, I

00:27:36.297 --> 00:27:37.630
would say, an engineer at heart.

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:41.470
And by that I mean
likes solving problems.

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:42.400
You know what I mean?

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:46.420
That like technology, that
think this stuff is just cool,

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:48.603
and that's certainly me and you.

00:27:48.603 --> 00:27:49.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:51.225
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
have one last question.

00:27:51.225 --> 00:27:52.600
We're almost
running out of time.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:54.400
But I have one more question.

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:57.550
You mentioned that the
speed at which companies

00:27:57.550 --> 00:28:00.310
are able to move to the
cloud depends mostly

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:03.610
on their willingness
or like how ready they

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.710
are as an organization to move.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:09.490
Is that for companies that
are doing basically, like, I

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:10.810
think it's lift and shift?

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:12.700
Or is it more like
are they rewriting

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:14.244
some of their components?

00:28:14.244 --> 00:28:15.910
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Yeah, obviously that's

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:16.870
a big variable.

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:17.620
Obviously that is.

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:21.150
If you're just taking
an existing system--

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:23.710
and obviously, no two
companies are alike.

00:28:23.710 --> 00:28:27.400
If you have a mainframe, things
are more complicated, right?

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:31.240
If you have SPARC
stations or other sorts

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:33.100
of strange hardware--
so I don't I

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:37.510
don't mean to say every
customer, if they just

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.010
willed it, could
move in the speed

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:41.060
that Lush or Evernote did.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:44.590
But I would say that
even large companies

00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:47.500
can have significant work
going on in the cloud

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:49.070
if they want to.

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:51.880
So that's not the same as saying
you've completely shut down

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:54.160
your data center, and
you're completely--

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:58.030
because, again, large
customers have large systems.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:00.810
But you can but you
can start to move now.

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:01.810
Do you know what I mean?

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:03.745
I think Eric Schmidt
said that at Cloud Next.

00:29:03.745 --> 00:29:05.780
Like, get to the cloud now.

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:07.990
That doesn't mean get
out of everything now.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:11.200
But it means start now.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.240
And this just comes up
over and over again.

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:16.150
Customers are fascinated
by artificial intelligence

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:17.440
and machine learning.

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.270
And my message to them
is the same thing.

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:21.010
Start investing now.

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:22.710
This is a different
way to program.

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:26.350
This is a different way to think
about application development.

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.260
You need to start now,
because you need to develop

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:30.170
the technical skills.

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:32.420
You need to understand what
it is you're trying to do.

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:36.550
So, yeah, I think Eric
Schmidt had it right.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:38.260
It almost doesn't
matter your industry.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:39.220
You can start now.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:39.970
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.530
All right, we are unfortunately
running out of time here.

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.602
But before we wrap
up, is there anything

00:29:44.602 --> 00:29:46.810
you want to make sure we
